to relay information from the far reaches of the empire back to the Senate.

253
00:14:45,360 --> 00:14:49,160
But lag time, sometimes as great as months from an outpost like Britain,

254
00:14:49,740 --> 00:14:53,920
prevented distant capitals like Rome from functioning as battle time command posts

255
00:14:53,921 --> 00:14:58,900
in outlying areas. Centralization was possible

256
00:14:58,901 --> 00:15:00,700
that Rome was the capital.

257
00:15:00,800 --> 00:15:04,740
And the Senate obviously ran the war

258
00:15:04,840 --> 00:15:08,140
because it sent the consuls out in order to execute that war.

259
00:15:08,240 --> 00:15:12,580
Very often, foreign policy was made on the borders.

260
00:15:12,680 --> 00:15:14,580
At a time when military commanders

261
00:15:14,680 --> 00:15:16,380
were among the few who were literate,

262
00:15:16,480 --> 00:15:19,120
written communication was a form of security,

263
00:15:19,220 --> 00:15:20,960
though it wasn't foolproof.

264
00:15:21,060 --> 00:15:23,620
Messages by courier could also be intercepted

265
00:15:23,720 --> 00:15:26,020
with monumental consequences.

266
00:15:26,021 --> 00:15:31,720
When Hannibal was in the south of Italy waiting for his brother to reinforce him,

267
00:15:31,721 --> 00:15:37,720
his brother sends a letter to Hannibal saying, let's meet up and we'll crush the Romans.

268
00:15:37,721 --> 00:15:40,314
Hannibal writes back, then the Romans intercepted.

269
00:15:40,315 --> 00:15:42,700
So now they know where his reinforcements are.

270
00:15:42,701 --> 00:15:48,580
They proceed to the north of Italy, defeat Hasdrubal, cut his head off, and then ride

271
00:15:48,581 --> 00:15:54,805
all the way down to the south of Italy and bowl the head into the tent of Hannibal.

272
00:15:54,806 --> 00:15:55,506
Sort of a

273
00:15:55,507 --> 00:15:59,480
reinforcements aren't coming. Securing intelligence

274
00:15:59,481 --> 00:16:03,480
sometimes required a disguise, a message scratched on tablets of

275
00:16:03,481 --> 00:16:08,480
lead and tied to a swimmer's arm for waterproof delivery, or

276
00:16:08,481 --> 00:16:12,480
etched on thin sheets of lead, then curled into a tube to be

277
00:16:12,481 --> 00:16:17,480
worn as a woman's earring, or tattooed on the shaved head of

278
00:16:17,481 --> 00:16:21,480
a slave and delivered only after his hair had grown back.

279
00:16:21,481 --> 00:16:24,480
The only message he was to deliver was shave my head.

280
00:16:24,481 --> 00:16:28,480
It would work with a military censor now.

281
00:16:28,481 --> 00:16:32,480
Military centralization didn't improve in the centuries following

282
00:16:32,481 --> 00:16:35,480
the Roman Empire. In fact, it declined during the

283
00:16:35,481 --> 00:16:39,480
Middle Ages as road systems fell apart and feudal hierarchy

284
00:16:39,481 --> 00:16:43,480
decentralized power, both political and military.

285
00:16:43,481 --> 00:16:47,480
You still had essentially communications that was, for the

286
00:16:47,481 --> 00:16:50,480
most part on the battlefield, no faster than your fastest

287
00:16:50,481 --> 00:16:53,480
rider. And that was the way of things

288
00:16:53,481 --> 00:16:56,380
things for some two millennia.

289
00:16:56,480 --> 00:16:58,980
But a decisive change was evident during the American

290
00:16:59,080 --> 00:17:03,420
Civil War due to two revolutionary technologies.

291
00:17:03,520 --> 00:17:07,520
The first was the telegraph, which for the first time since

292
00:17:07,620 --> 00:17:10,840
fire signals made distant communication possible without

293
00:17:10,940 --> 00:17:14,940
transporting a physical message.

294
00:17:15,040 --> 00:17:17,740
This is an example of a Civil War telegraph key.

295
00:17:17,840 --> 00:17:20,380
This particular example is called a camelback because of

296
00:17:20,381 --> 00:17:23,720
because of the hump of the lever arm for the key.

297
00:17:23,721 --> 00:17:26,860
What you would have here are a couple of contacts

298
00:17:26,861 --> 00:17:29,260
that once the contacts are closed,

299
00:17:29,261 --> 00:17:34,000
a spark is created, which travels along the line to a sounder.

300
00:17:34,001 --> 00:17:36,620
This would be at a distant station.

301
00:17:36,621 --> 00:17:39,560
And the spark goes through these coils here,

302
00:17:39,561 --> 00:17:42,140
and through induction makes this lever arm

303
00:17:42,141 --> 00:17:43,100
click against the other.

304
00:17:45,640 --> 00:17:50,140
Battlefield reports now traveled at electric speed.

305
00:17:50,141 --> 00:17:54,960
The dashes and dots of Morse code still needed to be translated, but distant commanders could

306
00:17:54,961 --> 00:17:57,960
coordinate quickly without leaving their command posts.

307
00:17:58,960 --> 00:18:03,900
The military telegraph was used to connect general headquarters to corps headquarters,

308
00:18:04,260 --> 00:18:08,200
down to division headquarters, but it usually didn't extend much farther than that, down

309
00:18:08,201 --> 00:18:12,260
to the company level, down to the foot soldier, the front lines, if you will.

310
00:18:13,000 --> 00:18:15,860
Below division commanders, communication was verbal.

311
00:18:15,861 --> 00:18:18,860
General Grant, who had a telegraph key at his command post,

312
00:18:18,960 --> 00:18:21,860
could send messages to President Lincoln in Washington

313
00:18:21,960 --> 00:18:24,860
faster than he could send messages to his own lieutenants.

314
00:18:24,960 --> 00:18:28,500
But the telegraph presented its own challenges.

315
00:18:28,600 --> 00:18:31,800
There are attempts to link division commanders

316
00:18:31,900 --> 00:18:34,320
with corps commanders with army commanders at Gettysburg

317
00:18:34,420 --> 00:18:35,820
by running telegraph wire.

318
00:18:35,920 --> 00:18:39,720
And the experiment was partially successful.

319
00:18:39,820 --> 00:18:43,860
A skilled telegrapher could send messages quite quickly,

320
00:18:43,861 --> 00:18:47,660
quickly, so you could get an answer back in 15 or 20 minutes

321
00:18:47,661 --> 00:18:50,900
to a query. A command post like

322
00:18:50,901 --> 00:18:54,700
Grant's, usually established in a nearby commandeered home or even

323
00:18:54,701 --> 00:18:59,500
a church, might have anywhere from 5 to 15 men, mostly clerks

324
00:18:59,501 --> 00:19:03,820
hand-copying orders. Others would gather information

325
00:19:03,821 --> 00:19:06,820
from couriers and dispatch it to officers.

326
00:19:06,821 --> 00:19:11,020
Still others would man the telegraph. Tables were used to

327
00:19:11,021 --> 00:19:15,420
maps of nearby battlefields. These maps might even include

328
00:19:15,421 --> 00:19:20,320
topographic information gathered by mapmaking scouts.

329
00:19:20,321 --> 00:19:22,660
The second revolutionary technology that changed the

330
00:19:22,661 --> 00:19:26,060
Civil War command post was the railroad.

331
00:19:26,061 --> 00:19:27,880
The railroad kind of drove the telegraph.

332
00:19:27,881 --> 00:19:30,780
In other words, with the railroad being able to move a

333
00:19:30,781 --> 00:19:33,320
large number of troops quickly, you had to have a means to get

334
00:19:33,321 --> 00:19:36,880
that word to the commander. A steam locomotive

335
00:19:36,881 --> 00:19:39,420
could sustain speeds far exceeding the top speed of a

336
00:19:39,421 --> 00:19:41,620
as long as the track was maintained,

337
00:19:41,621 --> 00:19:43,760
and the military could transport more on them

338
00:19:43,761 --> 00:19:45,620
than just couriers.

339
00:19:45,621 --> 00:19:47,960
It was able to utilize the railroad

340
00:19:47,961 --> 00:19:51,160
in moving large numbers of troops rapidly to the field.

341
00:19:51,161 --> 00:19:53,160
Jefferson Davis himself, for example,

342
00:19:53,161 --> 00:19:55,160
at the first Battle of Manassas,

343
00:19:55,161 --> 00:19:57,780
telegraphing the commander on the field

344
00:19:57,781 --> 00:20:02,880
to bring reinforcements, and they arrive by railroad.

345
00:20:02,881 --> 00:20:05,780
Command posts were able to collect remote information,

346
00:20:05,781 --> 00:20:09,420
evaluate it, then swiftly act with decisive force

347
00:20:09,421 --> 00:20:13,560
force. The north controlled more telegraph lines and railroad track

348
00:20:13,561 --> 00:20:17,020
than the south. So communication was a tactical

349
00:20:17,021 --> 00:20:20,920
advantage as long as the post could manage all the intelligence.

350
00:20:20,921 --> 00:20:25,360
And that required a staff of command post office workers.

351
00:20:25,361 --> 00:20:28,420
In addition to bureaucracy, centralization created another

352
00:20:28,421 --> 00:20:33,660
burden, at least from a military perspective, civilian oversight.

353
00:20:33,661 --> 00:20:38,960
These telegraph lines become very important to connecting Army

354
00:20:38,961 --> 00:20:43,000
field commanders to the Central Command in Washington

355
00:20:43,100 --> 00:20:44,560
at the War Department.

356
00:20:44,660 --> 00:20:48,460
That's where the telegraph lines all fed into.

357
00:20:48,560 --> 00:20:51,440
Curiously enough, they never continued the telegraph lines

358
00:20:51,540 --> 00:20:53,980
over to the White House just across the street.

359
00:20:54,080 --> 00:20:56,380
Abraham Lincoln would make his daily journeys

360
00:20:56,480 --> 00:20:59,040
from the White House and sit patiently,

361
00:20:59,140 --> 00:21:03,560
listening to the ticker machines, bring in the reports.

362
00:21:03,660 --> 00:21:05,860
As important as the telegraph and the railroad

363
00:21:05,960 --> 00:21:08,420
were to the evolution of command posts,

364
00:21:08,421 --> 00:21:11,320
they would both be eclipsed by innovations in communication

365
00:21:11,321 --> 00:21:14,860
technology that took place in the years following the end of

366
00:21:14,861 --> 00:21:20,460
the Civil War. Sending messages via carrier

367
00:21:20,461 --> 00:21:23,760
pigeons by exploiting their natural homing instincts has a

368
00:21:23,761 --> 00:21:27,180
long military history, but an inconsistent one for the United

369
00:21:27,181 --> 00:21:30,340
States. During World War I, U.S. carrier

370
00:21:30,341 --> 00:21:34,280
pigeons made excellent targets for enemy guns in Europe.

371
00:21:34,281 --> 00:21:38,080
Command Central will return on Modern Marvels.

372
00:21:38,420 --> 00:21:42,420
Command posts continued their path toward further

373
00:21:42,421 --> 00:21:47,420
centralization in part due to an 1876 invention.

374
00:21:47,421 --> 00:21:50,420
Alexander Graham Bell's telephone improved on the

375
00:21:50,421 --> 00:21:54,420
telegraph by sending a human voice along the cables rather

376
00:21:54,421 --> 00:21:58,420
than a coded blip. The phone made real-time two-way

377
00:21:58,421 --> 00:22:02,420
conversation possible over great distances. The military was

378
00:22:02,421 --> 00:22:07,420
eager to exploit its potential. By the 1880s, the United States

379
00:22:07,421 --> 00:22:12,020
the United States Army is pushing for a field telephone,

380
00:22:12,120 --> 00:22:14,460
and the person doing it is Adolphus Greeley.

381
00:22:14,560 --> 00:22:16,220
He develops the field telephone,

382
00:22:16,320 --> 00:22:18,940
and the United States Army uses it.

383
00:22:19,040 --> 00:22:21,940
Here you have a camp telephone of World War I vintage,

384
00:22:22,040 --> 00:22:25,500
a telephone he had developed by the start of the World War,

385
00:22:25,600 --> 00:22:28,000
and this was a new method of communication

386
00:22:28,100 --> 00:22:29,780
used within the front lines.

387
00:22:29,880 --> 00:22:31,520
You would have two telephone units,

388
00:22:31,620 --> 00:22:33,220
connected by a wire, of course,

389
00:22:33,320 --> 00:22:34,940
and if you wanted to make a call,

390
00:22:35,040 --> 00:22:36,780
you'd lift up on the handset here,

391
00:22:36,781 --> 00:22:39,820
over here and turn a crank, which would send a signal down

392
00:22:39,920 --> 00:22:41,780
the wire and ring a bell on the other end,

393
00:22:41,880 --> 00:22:44,720
alerting the other end that there was a call coming in.

394
00:22:44,820 --> 00:22:46,720
And once you got the call on the other line,

395
00:22:46,820 --> 00:22:49,220
you would just talk normally.

396
00:22:49,320 --> 00:22:53,260
A large battery inside the unit provided the power.

397
00:22:53,360 --> 00:22:55,760
The field telephone brought communication from command

398
00:22:55,860 --> 00:22:59,300
central to just behind the front.

399
00:22:59,400 --> 00:23:02,280
But because it needed a wire connection to a network,

400
00:23:02,380 --> 00:23:03,800
it was vulnerable to artillery.

401
00:23:03,801 --> 00:23:08,400
The logical improvement over the telegraph and telephone

402
00:23:08,500 --> 00:23:12,840
was real-time two-way communication without a landline.

403
00:23:12,940 --> 00:23:16,540
Marconi patented a wireless telegraph in 1895,

404
00:23:16,640 --> 00:23:18,740
precursor to the radio.

405
00:23:18,840 --> 00:23:21,960
But early wireless devices were big and heavy.

406
00:23:22,060 --> 00:23:24,460
Marconi's was practicable aboard ships

407
00:23:24,560 --> 00:23:26,460
where weight didn't make much difference

408
00:23:26,560 --> 00:23:31,460
and was adopted by the Navy by 1910 or 11.

409
00:23:31,461 --> 00:23:34,700
Ironically, World War I military command centers

410
00:23:34,800 --> 00:23:37,600
were able to communicate with naval ships far out at sea

411
00:23:37,700 --> 00:23:40,100
with relative ease using the radio.

412
00:23:40,200 --> 00:23:42,100
Yet communication with front line troops

413
00:23:42,200 --> 00:23:44,100
in the foxholes of eastern France

414
00:23:44,200 --> 00:23:46,100
was still impossible due to the size

415
00:23:46,200 --> 00:23:48,520
of the unwieldy devices.

416
00:23:48,620 --> 00:23:52,520
One reason why World War I offensives bogged down

417
00:23:52,620 --> 00:23:55,320
is because the infantry generally

418
00:23:55,420 --> 00:23:58,220
tries to advance in concert with the artillery,

419
00:23:58,320 --> 00:24:01,460
with the rolling barrages.

420
00:24:01,461 --> 00:24:04,700
But inevitably, there were snags.

421
00:24:04,800 --> 00:24:07,100
Barbed wire would slow infantrymen down.

422
00:24:07,200 --> 00:24:10,200
And so the artillery barrage, a time barrage,

423
00:24:10,300 --> 00:24:13,000
would roll forward, but the infantry

424
00:24:13,100 --> 00:24:15,000
would generally fall behind.

425
00:24:20,520 --> 00:24:22,820
More precisely, there was no quick way

426
00:24:22,920 --> 00:24:25,780
for the men in the trenches to communicate with the artillery

427
00:24:25,880 --> 00:24:28,060
via the command post to challenges

428
00:24:28,160 --> 00:24:29,620
they faced at the front.

429
00:24:29,621 --> 00:24:34,680
they faced at the front. Messengers proved too slow.

430
00:24:34,681 --> 00:24:38,120
But elsewhere in the theater of war, improvements in transportation

431
00:24:38,121 --> 00:24:41,680
were aiding military communication. The internal combustion

432
00:24:41,681 --> 00:24:45,060
engine paved the way for the automobile, faster than a courier's

433
00:24:45,061 --> 00:24:48,560
horse and more agile than a railroad locomotive.

434
00:24:48,561 --> 00:24:54,560
But it was the airplane's use of the internal combustion engine

435
00:24:54,561 --> 00:24:59,560
that really provided a leap forward in communication.

436
00:24:59,561 --> 00:25:02,160
The United States Army Signal Corps

437
00:25:02,161 --> 00:25:05,360
issues the first contract for an airplane,

438
00:25:05,361 --> 00:25:09,260
for a military airplane, with the Wright brothers in 1908.

439
00:25:09,261 --> 00:25:13,000
So this is hardly five years after the Wright brothers'

440
00:25:13,001 --> 00:25:14,000
first flight.

441
00:25:14,001 --> 00:25:15,900
But make no mistake, in the First World War,

442
00:25:15,901 --> 00:25:19,620
the key importance of the aircraft was reconnaissance.

443
00:25:19,621 --> 00:25:20,420
It wasn't bombing.

444
00:25:20,421 --> 00:25:21,320
It wasn't the fighter.

445
00:25:21,321 --> 00:25:23,320
It was reconnaissance.

446
00:25:23,321 --> 00:25:25,860
Reconnaissance planes provided a tactical advantage

447
00:25:25,861 --> 00:25:29,560
due to another technology invented in the 19th century.

448
00:25:29,561 --> 00:25:33,460
photographic camera. Because observers couldn't remember

449
00:25:33,461 --> 00:25:36,560
or sketch the labyrinthine trench system, photographs were

450
00:25:36,561 --> 00:25:39,560
the most important intelligence a World War I plane could

451
00:25:39,561 --> 00:25:43,560
capture. Photos and other data could be

452
00:25:43,561 --> 00:25:46,560
couriered by auto or flown back to General Pershing's

453
00:25:46,561 --> 00:25:50,560
headquarters in western France. Pershing and his staff

454
00:25:50,561 --> 00:25:53,560
created a command central out of commandeered French buildings

455
00:25:53,561 --> 00:25:57,560
behind the front. Couriers gave information from the

456
00:25:57,561 --> 00:26:02,560
sent the information on through telegraph, telephone, or radio

457
00:26:02,561 --> 00:26:05,560
connections up the chain of command to Washington if

458
00:26:05,561 --> 00:26:09,560
necessary. Sometimes information from above

459
00:26:09,561 --> 00:26:12,560
came down to Pershing's post, where his staff determined who

460
00:26:12,561 --> 00:26:16,560
needed to know what before sending it on its way.

461
00:26:16,561 --> 00:26:19,560
Reconnaissance photos, battlefield maps, intercepted

462
00:26:19,561 --> 00:26:22,560
enemy communications, requests for supplies, all passed

463
00:26:22,561 --> 00:26:26,560
through Pershing's command post, and were evaluated.

464
00:26:26,561 --> 00:26:30,560
and were evaluated. The command post had become a

465
00:26:30,561 --> 00:26:33,560
clearinghouse, sorting and prioritizing a deluge of

466
00:26:33,561 --> 00:26:36,560
intelligence. With overseas telegraph and

467
00:26:36,561 --> 00:26:39,560
telephone cables and radio relay stations connecting Pershing to

468
00:26:39,561 --> 00:26:42,560
Washington, the United States was on the verge of running the

469
00:26:42,561 --> 00:26:45,560
war thousands of miles from the battlefield.

470
00:26:45,561 --> 00:26:48,560
The Army Signal Corps in World War I connected Washington,

471
00:26:48,561 --> 00:26:52,560
D.C. with Pershing's headquarters in France, with the

472
00:26:52,561 --> 00:27:00,360
With the divisional commanders, in other words, Washington could talk to the divisional commanders if Washington so chose to do that.

473
00:27:01,060 --> 00:27:02,660
But Washington chose not to.

474
00:27:02,661 --> 00:27:10,280
One reason why Washington wasn't running the war is that Woodrow Wilson did not particularly feel that he was capable of running the war.

475
00:27:10,580 --> 00:27:13,280
So essentially Washington gave Pershing carte blanche.

476
00:27:13,900 --> 00:27:21,000
But certainly this communications net enabled Pershing to immediately transmit requests for necessities.

477
00:27:21,001 --> 00:27:24,000
military professionals hoped political leaders would trust

478
00:27:24,001 --> 00:27:28,000
the system. Even after the armistice in 1918,

479
00:27:28,001 --> 00:27:31,000
military planners continued improving communications

480
00:27:31,001 --> 00:27:35,000
technology, like the telephone and the radio, strengthening the

481
00:27:35,001 --> 00:27:40,000
capabilities of command posts for the anticipated next war.

482
00:27:40,001 --> 00:27:44,000
And by World War II, new technology was finally able to

483
00:27:44,001 --> 00:27:48,000
connect Washington, D.C. to the commanders at the front lines.

484
00:27:48,001 --> 00:27:52,000
Now this is a SignalCore BC-611 transceiver.

485
00:27:52,001 --> 00:27:55,000
What you would do with this is after you've jumped with it,

486
00:27:55,001 --> 00:27:57,000
a lot of times paratroopers jump with it

487
00:27:57,001 --> 00:28:00,000
and they have this cover to keep you from inadvertently

488
00:28:00,001 --> 00:28:03,000
sending out a signal, you take the cover off.

489
00:28:05,000 --> 00:28:08,000
You would take off the antenna cover,

490
00:28:11,000 --> 00:28:15,000
extend the antenna, and the act of extending the antenna

491
00:28:15,001 --> 00:28:17,000
actually turns the unit on.

492
00:28:17,001 --> 00:28:21,800
and then press the PTT switch to push to talk switch

493
00:28:21,801 --> 00:28:23,140
and talk into this normally.

494
00:28:23,720 --> 00:28:25,120
Release the switch to receive.

495
00:28:26,200 --> 00:28:29,000
You have a sling so you can put it over your shoulder

496
00:28:29,001 --> 00:28:31,720
so you can be looking at a map or whatever

497
00:28:31,721 --> 00:28:33,640
while you're using it or sling it over your back

498
00:28:33,641 --> 00:28:34,341
when not in use.

499
00:28:34,680 --> 00:28:37,240
Turn the unit off, push the antenna back down

500
00:28:37,241 --> 00:28:37,941
into its housing.

501
00:28:42,020 --> 00:28:45,320
This is a BC-1000 walkie-talkie.

502
00:28:45,321 --> 00:28:50,320
This is an FM radio as opposed to the AM transceiver that we just looked at.

503
00:28:50,321 --> 00:28:54,320
This has greater range than the handheld transceiver,

504
00:28:54,321 --> 00:28:59,320
and it also is less resistant to interference, thanks to the frequency modulation.

505
00:28:59,321 --> 00:29:03,320
To operate this, you would tune in your frequency here.

506
00:29:03,321 --> 00:29:07,320
Again, this is an advantage over the handheld, which is a fixed frequency.

507
00:29:07,321 --> 00:29:09,320
This is a tunable frequency.

508
00:29:09,321 --> 00:29:12,320
You have a handset right here.

509
00:29:12,321 --> 00:29:15,760
when you talk, you rotate this little lever with your thumb

510
00:29:15,860 --> 00:29:20,820
to talk and then release to receive.

511
00:29:20,920 --> 00:29:22,960
The knob switched the circuit from the receiver

512
00:29:23,060 --> 00:29:24,960
attached to the earphone to the transmitter

513
00:29:25,060 --> 00:29:30,080
in the mouthpiece. Army Chief of Staff George Marshall

514
00:29:30,180 --> 00:29:32,880
embraced technology, which allowed him to conduct from

515
00:29:32,980 --> 00:29:36,420
Washington a single war in two regions, thousands of miles

516
00:29:36,520 --> 00:29:41,780
apart, the European Theater and the Pacific Theater.

517
00:29:41,781 --> 00:29:49,220
This idea of separate theaters of war managed simultaneously from far away was possible only because of improved communication.

518
00:29:50,440 --> 00:29:53,940
Yet Marshall gave his regional commanders enormous autonomy.

519
00:29:53,941 --> 00:30:03,560
If you look at Marshall's administrative philosophy, it's something like that of a very large, well-run corporation with branch offices.

520
00:30:03,561 --> 00:30:07,260
And the boss at the center, if he is good,

521
00:30:07,800 --> 00:30:10,300
has excellent information flow

522
00:30:10,301 --> 00:30:12,200
from the heads of those branch offices.

523
00:30:12,900 --> 00:30:15,260
Eisenhower called the War Department in Washington

524
00:30:15,261 --> 00:30:17,720
from his European Theater command post in France

525
00:30:17,721 --> 00:30:21,320
days after the June 6, 1944 D-Day invasion

526
00:30:21,321 --> 00:30:23,200
to order vital tank parts.

527
00:30:25,220 --> 00:30:26,980
Marshall was also in close communication

528
00:30:26,981 --> 00:30:29,180
with General MacArthur and Admiral Nimitz,

529
00:30:29,600 --> 00:30:31,040
both managing the Pacific Theater.

530
00:30:31,041 --> 00:30:33,680
But on the other side of the front,

531
00:30:33,780 --> 00:30:36,340
there was a perfect illustration of the flaw inherent

532
00:30:36,440 --> 00:30:38,640
in a centralized command structure,

533
00:30:38,740 --> 00:30:41,380
a micromanaging dictator at the very center

534
00:30:41,480 --> 00:30:43,680
of a centralized command.

535
00:30:43,780 --> 00:30:46,980
Hitler is the perfect example of a national leader

536
00:30:47,080 --> 00:30:51,700
who concerned himself not only with the movement of armies,

537
00:30:51,800 --> 00:30:53,500
but with the movement of divisions,

538
00:30:53,600 --> 00:30:57,400
with the movement of units of brigade size, battalion size,

539
00:30:57,401 --> 00:31:01,040
battalion size, that is, units of fewer than 1,000 men.

540
00:31:01,140 --> 00:31:06,200
And Hitler's interference, which this modern communications made

541
00:31:06,300 --> 00:31:10,780
possible, was a great advantage to us.

542
00:31:10,880 --> 00:31:13,580
Communications technology enabled the World War II

543
00:31:13,680 --> 00:31:17,060
command post to monitor battlefields across the globe.

544
00:31:17,160 --> 00:31:19,280
But the next war would, for the most part,

545
00:31:19,380 --> 00:31:23,500
be a cold one, based on high-tech spying, greater

546
00:31:23,501 --> 00:31:27,100
centralization, and more bureaucracy in the command

547
00:31:27,101 --> 00:31:32,300
structure. As a captain during World War I,

548
00:31:32,301 --> 00:31:35,640
future General George Marshall so impressed General Pershing

549
00:31:35,641 --> 00:31:37,940
that after the armistice, Pershing made him his aide

550
00:31:37,941 --> 00:31:40,960
to camp. Command Central will return on

551
00:31:40,961 --> 00:31:47,640
Modern Marvels. After World War II, the United

552
00:31:47,641 --> 00:31:50,840
States was the dominant Western power against the Communists in

553
00:31:50,841 --> 00:31:55,540
subsequent Cold War. Now Secretary of State, George

554
00:31:55,541 --> 00:31:58,040
Marshall worked with President Truman to create a new

555
00:31:58,041 --> 00:32:02,120
centralized military based on regional commands connected by

556
00:32:02,121 --> 00:32:05,560
superior communication technology. His changes began

557
00:32:05,561 --> 00:32:09,720
at the top. He began to back what we would

558
00:32:09,721 --> 00:32:12,920
call the Defense Department, that is a single unified

559
00:32:12,921 --> 00:32:16,760
cabinet level department in which all the military branches

560
00:32:16,761 --> 00:32:20,600
would be under a single civilian chief.

561
00:32:20,601 --> 00:32:22,560
Under Truman, Marshall reorganized

562
00:32:22,561 --> 00:32:25,400
the Joint Chiefs of Staff, representing the different branches

563
00:32:25,401 --> 00:32:28,140
of the military, to answer to the newly created

564
00:32:28,141 --> 00:32:31,800
Secretary of Defense. The Secretary of Defense

565
00:32:31,801 --> 00:32:35,480
answered only to the president. Eventually, as the unified

566
00:32:35,481 --> 00:32:39,040
command posts we know today evolved, they too would answer

567
00:32:39,041 --> 00:32:41,880
directly to the Secretary of Defense.

568
00:32:41,881 --> 00:32:45,480
So while Marshall does not invent the modern army's

569
00:32:45,481 --> 00:32:47,480
organizational structure.

570
00:32:47,580 --> 00:32:51,880
He certainly is the man who was at least as important as anyone

571
00:32:51,980 --> 00:32:56,720
else in showing the way that it should be done.

572
00:32:56,820 --> 00:32:58,820
Technologies that would support and enhance

573
00:32:58,920 --> 00:33:02,100
this new command structure were embodied in the computer

574
00:33:02,200 --> 00:33:05,240
and the satellite.

575
00:33:05,340 --> 00:33:07,760
Our need to have rapid communications

576
00:33:07,860 --> 00:33:11,840
was, of course, pushed very hard by the threat

577
00:33:11,940 --> 00:33:14,840
of a Soviet surprise attack on the United States,

578
00:33:14,841 --> 00:33:18,180
a nuclear Pearl Harbor, a decapitation strike

579
00:33:18,280 --> 00:33:20,840
against the leadership in Washington, D.C.

580
00:33:20,940 --> 00:33:24,680
by big Soviet ICBMs might well take out

581
00:33:24,780 --> 00:33:27,780
the entire National Command Authority.

582
00:33:27,880 --> 00:33:30,180
Satellites offered instant early detection,

583
00:33:30,280 --> 00:33:33,600
important in a Soviet ICBM attack that might take only 30

584
00:33:33,700 --> 00:33:35,100
minutes.

585
00:33:35,200 --> 00:33:38,300
We need the ability to look into the Soviet Union

586
00:33:38,400 --> 00:33:41,200
and discern what their intentions are.

587
00:33:41,300 --> 00:33:43,440
It was important to get satellites up,

588
00:33:43,441 --> 00:33:48,840
And we achieved that by the early 1960s.

589
00:33:48,841 --> 00:33:52,440
Monitoring events throughout the world was easier with a giant

590
00:33:52,441 --> 00:33:55,780
eye floating overhead that could beam information back to

591
00:33:55,781 --> 00:33:58,280
Washington and its military command posts.

592
00:33:58,281 --> 00:34:01,800
Now those military posts needed a giant brain.

593
00:34:01,801 --> 00:34:05,100
Early computers were already tabulating artillery accuracy

594
00:34:05,101 --> 00:34:07,700
during the later stages of World War II.

595
00:34:07,701 --> 00:34:11,040
By the 1950s, they were working to collect and store

596
00:34:11,041 --> 00:34:13,440
intelligence. You need the help of

597
00:34:13,441 --> 00:34:15,459
computers to help process all this data.

598
00:34:15,460 --> 00:34:18,640
It's more data than a human can get through in a timely manner.

599
00:34:18,641 --> 00:34:23,440
In late 1950, the military created ARPA, the Advanced Research Projects Agency,

600
00:34:23,441 --> 00:34:26,819
and its job was to develop new communications technologies.

601
00:34:26,820 --> 00:34:28,480
Well, ARPA scientists fiddled

602
00:34:28,481 --> 00:34:35,280
around with computers, linked them together, and by 1969, they had four nodes connected together

603
00:34:35,281 --> 00:34:37,447
in what they called an ARPANET.

604
00:34:37,448 --> 00:34:41,920
Now, from this ARPANET grew more nodes until it evolved into the

605
00:34:41,921 --> 00:34:45,160
internet that we know it today. These innovations made

606
00:34:45,161 --> 00:34:48,420
communication ever faster and more complete.

607
00:34:48,421 --> 00:34:52,320
Photographs could be sent via satellite, as could television

608
00:34:52,321 --> 00:34:56,120
images. Messages could be broadcast from

609
00:34:56,121 --> 00:34:59,680
one computer to another. The need to physically courier a

610
00:34:59,681 --> 00:35:02,580
message was long gone, but weaknesses in the system

611
00:35:02,581 --> 00:35:05,320
weren't. During the war in Vietnam in the

612
00:35:05,321 --> 00:35:09,960
1960s, opponents with fewer resources and less technology

613
00:35:09,961 --> 00:35:14,400
exploited them by fighting low-tech guerrilla-style wars.

614
00:35:14,500 --> 00:35:19,640
They have adopted a very fine cell-like system that

615
00:35:19,740 --> 00:35:22,440
suppresses the flow of information.

616
00:35:22,540 --> 00:35:25,440
And this makes it difficult for a technologically oriented

617
00:35:25,540 --> 00:35:29,620
society like ours to break into that.

618
00:35:29,720 --> 00:35:32,380
America continued to pursue a more centralized regional

619
00:35:32,480 --> 00:35:35,680
command structure, even as the Cold War began to thaw in the

620
00:35:35,780 --> 00:35:39,620
1980s and 1990s. But events in the Near East and

621
00:35:39,621 --> 00:35:42,920
in North Africa, plus the strategic importance of oil,

622
00:35:43,020 --> 00:35:46,820
refocused attention toward that region of the world.

623
00:35:46,920 --> 00:35:48,360
Before the creation of Central Command,

624
00:35:48,460 --> 00:35:50,760
the world was divided up between the European command,

625
00:35:50,860 --> 00:35:54,560
which stretched as far as Pakistan and Afghanistan,

626
00:35:54,660 --> 00:35:58,380
and the Pacific command, which actually included Pakistan,

627
00:35:58,480 --> 00:36:02,780
and India, and the dividing line between the commands

628
00:36:02,880 --> 00:36:05,180
was at that border.

629
00:36:05,280 --> 00:36:07,480
A long war between Iran and Iraq

630
00:36:07,481 --> 00:36:11,480
Soviet invasion of Afghanistan and the Iranian hostage crisis,

631
00:36:11,481 --> 00:36:15,480
both in 1979. President Carter created the

632
00:36:15,481 --> 00:36:18,480
Rapid Deployment Task Force, which monitored these Middle

633
00:36:18,481 --> 00:36:22,480
Eastern events and was on standby to intervene militarily.

634
00:36:22,481 --> 00:36:25,480
Then when Reagan became president, he decided that the

635
00:36:25,481 --> 00:36:28,480
best thing to do with this rather ad hoc unit called the

636
00:36:28,481 --> 00:36:32,480
Rapid Deployment Joint Task Force was to turn it into a

637
00:36:32,481 --> 00:36:36,480
full-fledged unified command in the same sense as the European

638
00:36:36,481 --> 00:36:39,720
the European Command, the Pacific Command.

639
00:36:39,820 --> 00:36:44,420
Central Command became a full command in 1983.

640
00:36:44,520 --> 00:36:47,520
CENTCOM's stewardship of the region was put to the test

641
00:36:47,620 --> 00:36:52,720
when in 1990 Saddam Hussein's Iraq invaded Kuwait.

642
00:36:52,820 --> 00:36:56,340
Again, the world's eyes were riveted to this volatile region

643
00:36:56,440 --> 00:36:59,040
under Central Command's watch.

644
00:36:59,140 --> 00:37:02,040
But what really set things into motion

645
00:37:02,140 --> 00:37:05,480
was the US decision to send over half a million troops,

646
00:37:05,481 --> 00:37:14,420
plus the troops of some 30-odd other coalition members to invade Iraq and Kuwait and take back Kuwait from the Iraqis in 1991.

647
00:37:15,200 --> 00:37:22,700
This put the command on a totally different footing, and from that level of strategic attention flowed the new technologies,

648
00:37:22,701 --> 00:37:29,540
the innovative military experiences, and other advantages, if you will, over the 1990s.

649
00:37:29,541 --> 00:37:33,540
The Gulf War was also the last war in a long line of American

650
00:37:33,541 --> 00:37:37,220
military engagements before communications technology truly

651
00:37:37,221 --> 00:37:42,740
permeated every aspect of warfare. The Gulf War of 1991 was in

652
00:37:42,741 --> 00:37:46,740
many ways the Cold War battle that we never fought against the

653
00:37:46,741 --> 00:37:50,340
Soviets. And we got glimpses of the future

654
00:37:50,341 --> 00:37:53,300
of an information technology-driven war, but it was not

655
00:37:53,301 --> 00:37:55,700
one. It was a war that was still fought

656
00:37:55,701 --> 00:37:57,700
mostly by tank ships and planes.

657
00:37:57,701 --> 00:38:01,940
And so the modern wars that started with the Civil War

658
00:38:01,941 --> 00:38:04,040
and ended with the Gulf War, really wars that

659
00:38:04,041 --> 00:38:06,980
were characterized by industrial mass,

660
00:38:06,981 --> 00:38:11,420
and World War II really being the apogee of that style

661
00:38:11,421 --> 00:38:12,420
of warfare.

662
00:38:12,421 --> 00:38:14,180
But if you look at wars of the information age,

663
00:38:14,181 --> 00:38:20,820
in World War II, what it took 1,000 bombers to destroy,

664
00:38:20,821 --> 00:38:23,400
it'd be destroyed by 100 bombers in Vietnam.

665
00:38:25,700 --> 00:38:30,700
could be destroyed by maybe 10 bombers in the 1991 Gulf War

666
00:38:30,800 --> 00:38:34,700
and can now be destroyed by one bomber or even less

667
00:38:34,800 --> 00:38:38,480
in the 2003 Iraq War because of the advances in information

668
00:38:38,580 --> 00:38:41,480
technology and communications, not so much the advances

669
00:38:41,580 --> 00:38:43,480
in weaponry.

670
00:38:43,580 --> 00:38:47,480
The war in Iraq that began on March 19, 2003,

671
00:38:47,580 --> 00:38:51,800
was a new war for a new era, managed by computer and telephone

672
00:38:51,801 --> 00:38:55,040
telephones, and video conferences from command posts

673
00:38:55,041 --> 00:38:58,040
hundreds, even thousands of miles away.

674
00:38:58,041 --> 00:39:01,040
Communication technology no longer simply helped relay

675
00:39:01,041 --> 00:39:03,640
messages and gather intelligence.

676
00:39:03,641 --> 00:39:07,560
Now it made bombs smarter, tanks deadlier, and the fighting

677
00:39:07,561 --> 00:39:13,840
force smaller. Of the nine unified commands,

678
00:39:13,841 --> 00:39:17,240
Strategic Command is responsible for space surveillance or

679
00:39:17,241 --> 00:39:20,760
monitoring man-made objects orbiting Earth.

680
00:39:20,761 --> 00:39:29,560
will return on Modern Marvels. The future of the command post

681
00:39:29,561 --> 00:39:33,560
is virtual, a command post that exists in the web of electronic

682
00:39:33,561 --> 00:39:37,960
communications, rather than a real place of brick and mortar.

683
00:39:37,961 --> 00:39:41,560
In future wars, computers will allow soldiers and battlefield

684
00:39:41,561 --> 00:39:45,560
commanders to pull down from this virtual command post their own

685
00:39:45,561 --> 00:39:48,760
options based on their environment, rather than having

686
00:39:48,761 --> 00:39:51,660
thrust upon them. We will design systems

687
00:39:51,661 --> 00:39:55,000
where the user will be able to determine, much like the users

688
00:39:55,001 --> 00:39:57,400
of the internet do, what they want to see.

689
00:39:57,401 --> 00:40:01,000
So the broadcaster won't be in charge of what information goes

690
00:40:01,001 --> 00:40:03,900
to the user. The user will be in charge.

691
00:40:03,901 --> 00:40:06,320
In future wars, information will become

692
00:40:06,321 --> 00:40:09,720
the primary weapon. In future wars, soldiers may even

693
00:40:09,721 --> 00:40:12,820
remain back at the command post, safely removed from the

694
00:40:12,821 --> 00:40:17,320
battlefield. Why send a soldier or a pilot

695
00:40:17,321 --> 00:40:20,320
somewhere where you can send an unmanned vehicle that can

696
00:40:20,420 --> 00:40:22,560
provide the same sort of information, in fact,

697
00:40:22,660 --> 00:40:25,320
better information, because an unmanned vehicle not only

698
00:40:25,420 --> 00:40:28,700
can have several different kinds of sensory devices

699
00:40:28,800 --> 00:40:32,000
built into it, but it only exposes the technology to

700
00:40:32,100 --> 00:40:35,380
danger when it gets in trouble and not a human life.

701
00:40:35,480 --> 00:40:38,240
Technology can now bring a realistic virtual version

702
00:40:38,340 --> 00:40:42,580
of the battlefield right to the command post.

703
00:40:42,680 --> 00:40:45,840
Examples of this futuristic net-centric warfare

704
00:40:45,841 --> 00:40:50,280
were on display during the invasion of Iraq in 2003.

705
00:40:50,380 --> 00:40:53,540
Look at the second Gulf War, where daily you're faxing

706
00:40:53,640 --> 00:40:55,420
a large number of things back, and everybody

707
00:40:55,520 --> 00:40:57,460
is on instant messaging, and everybody's

708
00:40:57,560 --> 00:41:01,160
emailing back with regularity.

709
00:41:01,260 --> 00:41:04,360
That changed the whole approach to technology

710
00:41:04,460 --> 00:41:05,660
in less than a decade.

711
00:41:05,760 --> 00:41:08,500
I think sometimes we forget that in the first Gulf War,

712
00:41:08,600 --> 00:41:11,860
we didn't even have email.

713
00:41:11,960 --> 00:41:15,140
In both Afghanistan and in Iraq recently,

714
00:41:15,141 --> 00:41:18,980
you had the specter of B-2 bombers running their daily

715
00:41:19,080 --> 00:41:22,480
bombing missions from Missouri to Afghanistan and Iraq and back

716
00:41:22,580 --> 00:41:26,580
again, controlled by headquarters from Florida,

717
00:41:26,680 --> 00:41:30,180
and supplied by information gathered maybe another continent

718
00:41:30,280 --> 00:41:34,000
away and sent to them in real time.

719
00:41:34,100 --> 00:41:37,000
And that network will only get more powerful.

720
00:41:37,100 --> 00:41:40,000
Global positioning systems may someday be sewn into every

721
00:41:40,100 --> 00:41:41,300
uniform.

722
00:41:41,400 --> 00:41:45,140
Video cameras may someday be installed into every helmet.

723
00:41:45,141 --> 00:41:48,580
computer programs can generate nearly infinite scenarios for

724
00:41:48,581 --> 00:41:53,580
how a conflict might play out. The technology exists, but the

725
00:41:53,581 --> 00:41:56,700
flow of information may become overwhelming.

726
00:41:56,701 --> 00:41:58,700
Even though we have the ability to get things from

727
00:41:58,701 --> 00:42:02,500
satellites and to electronically monitor information, it

728
00:42:02,501 --> 00:42:04,940
complicates things because we wind up with more information

729
00:42:04,941 --> 00:42:08,300
than we can analyze at any given time. And there's always a

730
00:42:08,301 --> 00:42:12,580
possibility of, if not human error, human sabotage getting

731
00:42:12,581 --> 00:42:15,140
into one of those systems. And what if the

732
00:42:15,141 --> 00:42:18,140
the whole thing crashes.

733
00:42:18,141 --> 00:42:21,380
An astute adversary will go after this advantage,

734
00:42:21,381 --> 00:42:23,520
whether it's through computer viruses

735
00:42:23,521 --> 00:42:25,820
or whether it's through electromagnetic weapons

736
00:42:25,821 --> 00:42:28,260
or whether it's through any means meant

737
00:42:28,261 --> 00:42:31,720
to disrupt this big network of communications that sits

738
00:42:31,721 --> 00:42:34,920
like a bubble above American forces.

739
00:42:34,921 --> 00:42:36,700
Another option for adversaries

740
00:42:36,701 --> 00:42:39,800
is to completely slip under the radar, so to speak,

741
00:42:39,801 --> 00:42:42,100
to play by rules other than the ones dictated

742
00:42:42,101 --> 00:42:45,700
communication systems. The real key to success in

743
00:42:45,701 --> 00:42:49,540
warfare is to get to the future ahead of the next guy.

744
00:42:49,541 --> 00:42:53,380
And on September 11, 2001, for instance, you could say that

745
00:42:53,381 --> 00:42:56,580
al-Qaeda arrived at the future before we did.

746
00:42:59,380 --> 00:43:04,020
They brought a new style of warfare to our shores.

747
00:43:08,500 --> 00:43:11,060
Not every challenge faced by the United States

748
00:43:11,061 --> 00:43:13,460
can be handled through an email from a command post

749
00:43:13,560 --> 00:43:15,860
thousands of miles away.

750
00:43:15,960 --> 00:43:18,560
It took a very low-tech approach

751
00:43:18,660 --> 00:43:21,700
to intelligence gathering to find Saddam Hussein.

752
00:43:21,800 --> 00:43:25,800
So at what point do we listen to our technical instincts,

753
00:43:25,900 --> 00:43:27,820
which allow us to draw further and further away

754
00:43:27,920 --> 00:43:31,620
from the battlefield, or do we listen to our political instincts,

755
00:43:31,720 --> 00:43:34,580
which require us to have very physical engagements

756
00:43:34,680 --> 00:43:38,420
in the sorts of missions that CENTCOM's involved in these days?

757
00:43:41,060 --> 00:43:44,900
Modern wars, now monitored by command posts like CENTCOM,

758
00:43:44,901 --> 00:43:48,100
combine both technological and human components.

759
00:43:48,101 --> 00:43:51,100
Even the communications technology utilized by modern

760
00:43:51,101 --> 00:43:55,700
commands has to be read, interpreted, and acted upon by a

761
00:43:55,701 --> 00:43:59,220
person. At the end of the day,

762
00:43:59,221 --> 00:44:03,220
war is a human business. All commanders bring a

763
00:44:03,221 --> 00:44:07,220
different style of command. Some are very, very comfortable

764
00:44:07,221 --> 00:44:10,720
handling it through pure technology, and perhaps have had

765
00:44:10,721 --> 00:44:13,460
the training time to understand all that.

766
00:44:13,461 --> 00:44:17,600
Others like a mixture of it. I like to look a subordinate in

767
00:44:17,601 --> 00:44:20,260
the eyes to understand his confidence.

768
00:44:20,261 --> 00:44:23,900
Does he understand my intent? And then you've always got the

769
00:44:23,901 --> 00:44:28,780
technological piece to back it up. And if the long history of

770
00:44:28,781 --> 00:44:33,420
command post is any indication, that technological component will

771
00:44:33,421 --> 00:44:37,420
get more sophisticated in the future.

